{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Reached.</b> From the nifal binyan, except it is a participle; so too \"you reached the kingship.\"" "<b>Mityahadim.</b> A foreign word. Rabbi Jonah the grammarian said: from this word we learn that the word תורמין is correct, for the yod of יהודה is not a root letter — and behold, in the word מתיהדים the shin is the root. He further said: מוח ומלוח is like \"your going out and your coming in,\" for he would expect it to be מבואך, only it comes in the manner of its companions. He did not say outright that the vav of מובאך, if added, should not surprise me, for the letters alef, he, vav, yod are found added — whether at the beginning of a word, or in the middle, or at the end — and they are also absent; but it is not so with the mem of מליח. The answer regarding תורמין is what our Rabbis of blessed memory said: the language of Scripture is one thing, and the language of the Talmud is another. It is also possible that מתיהדים means they traced their lineage to the tribe of Judah — and this would be grammatically sound."], :book :esther, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {1 "Read שב as שן ('tooth'), i.e. the letter shin, based on the phonological argument about root letters that follows. The alternative reading 'in the word מתיהדים the shin is the root' is not viable since shin is not the root of מתיהדים."}, :source-witnesses [["Kol Sason, Krotoschin, 1840" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001728315"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:30:24.457290215Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}